---

<section id="formula" class="py-16 bg-slate-900/40 border-y border-slate-800/80">
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center">
      <!-- Explanation Text -->
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-brand-500/10 text-brand-400 text-xs font-mono border border-brand-500/20"
        >
          Physics Principles
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
          How to Convert Kilograms to Litres
        </h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
          Converting mass (kilograms) to liquid volume (litres) is not a direct 1:1 ratio for every
          liquid. While <strong>1 kg of pure water equals exactly 1 litre</strong> (at 4°C), other substances
          possess distinct mass densities due to their molecular structures.
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl glass-card border border-slate-800 flex items-start gap-4">
            <div
              class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-brand-500/20 text-brand-400 flex items-center justify-center font-bold font-mono"
            >
              1
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-semibold text-white text-sm">To Convert Kg to Litres</h4>
              <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-0.5">
                Divide the total weight in kilograms by the liquid density (&rho;) in kg/L.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl glass-card border border-slate-800 flex items-start gap-4">
            <div
              class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/20 text-sky-400 flex items-center justify-center font-bold font-mono"
            >
              2
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-semibold text-white text-sm">To Convert Litres to Kg</h4>
              <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-0.5">
                Multiply the total liquid volume in litres by the substance density (&rho;) in kg/L.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Math Display Cards -->
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <!-- Formula Box 1 -->
        <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-brand-500/30 glow-brand space-y-4">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold text-brand-400 uppercase tracking-wider"
            >Formula 1: Kilograms to Litres</span
          >
          <div
            class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-950 font-mono text-center text-lg sm:text-xl text-white border border-slate-800"
          >
            Volume (L) = Mass (kg) &divide; Density (kg/L)
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-normal">
            Where <strong>Volume</strong> is in Litres (L), <strong>Mass</strong> is in Kilograms (kg),
            and <strong>&rho; (rho)</strong> is density in kg/L.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Formula Box 2 -->
        <div class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 border border-slate-800 space-y-4">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold text-sky-400 uppercase tracking-wider"
            >Formula 2: Litres to Kilograms</span
          >
          <div
            class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-950 font-mono text-center text-lg sm:text-xl text-white border border-slate-800"
          >
            Mass (kg) = Volume (L) &times; Density (kg/L)
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-normal">
            Example: 5 Litres of Olive Oil (&rho; = 0.918 kg/L) = 5 &times; 0.918 = 4.59 kg.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
